SparkyBin Browser is a fork of GNOME Web (Epiphany). The changes made relative to
upstream 51.1 are recorded in the NOTICE file.

0.2 - October 20, 2026
=====================

* The sidebar is new. It shows either the bookmarks or the open tabs, and
  a switch at the top of it says which. The bookmarks are in groups, and
  the groups are the user's to choose and to order in the preferences.

* Fifteen colour palettes are available, named after the projects they come
  from: Gruvbox, Dracula, Catppuccin, Nord, Tokyo Night, Solarized, One
  Dark, Rose Pine, Kanagawa, Everforest, Monokai Pro, Ayu Mirage and
  Poimandres.

* The browser keeps its own sizes now, so that a theme cannot squash it.
  A theme was previously free to decide that a header bar had no height.

* An installed ad blocker now does something. WebKit has no way to let an
  extension stop a request by itself, so the browser compiles the filter
  lists that come with an extension into the rules that WebKit's content
  blockers understand, and puts them on the pages. It is the arrangement
  Safari uses, and it is what makes the whole of uBlock Origin's list of
  filters work here. The lists are compiled once and kept.

* New WebExtension calls: permissions.contains and getAll,
  runtime.onStartup and onInstalled, browserAction.setTitle and setIcon,
  tabs.onUpdated and onActivated.

* Fixed a number of crashes in the WebExtension code, all of which took
  the whole browser down: the JSON quoting helper, the extension's own
  schemes not being addresses, the extension having no identifier, a
  missing translation, and a reference to the bookmarks manager that was
  freed twice.

0.1 - October 19, 2026
======================

First release of SparkyBin Browser, forked from GNOME Web 51.1.

* Changes relative to upstream 51.1:
  - Rebranded to SparkyBin Browser, with a new application ID, a new
    executable name and user data directories that no longer collide with a
    GNOME Web installation
  - WebExtensions are enabled by default
  - Fixed a number of crashes, use-after-frees and security issues in the
    WebExtension implementation, including a JavaScript injection in
    downloads.search(), a path traversal in downloads.download() and the
    extension API being handed to cross-origin frames
